[{"contents": [{"content": true, "type": "has_nav"}, {"content": true, "type": "has_exercise_controls"}], "type": "lab_options"}], "stimuli": [{"_data": [], "contents": [{}], "template_override": 1, "id": 0, "type": "welcome"}, {"template_override": 1, "_data": [], "type": "audio", "title": "", "id": 1, "contents": [{"value": {"coverimg": "enpe7n2le02ob11re2img01.jpg", "images": [{"time": "0", "text": "<span class=\"strong\">Kyle</span>: After reading this article, I feel like I wasted my time because I didn't understand anything. <br/>\n<span class=\"strong\">Mandy</span>: What are you reading about? Maybe I can help you. <br/>\n<span class=\"strong\">Kyle</span>: It's about structured programming. You know, history, Dijkstra, and the principles. I hope you can help me. Sometimes I need to ask for help. <br/>\n<span class=\"strong\">Mandy</span>: Sure! Before, computers weren't so complex. When scientists started to implement more functions, computers and software became more difficult to understand. So, in 1968, the scientist Edsger Dijkstra said something that changed programming forever. His role in programming is very important. Are you familiar with that name? <br/>", "image": "enpe7n2le02ob11re2img01.jpg", "id": 0}, {"time": "43000", "text": "<span class=\"strong\">Kyle</span>: Yes! Dijkstra stated that the \"GO TO\" statement wasn't beneficial for programming. Those statements were harmful because they made the logic of the operation more difficult to understand. <br/>\n<span class=\"strong\">Mandy</span>: That's right! I can see you know about this. Sometimes, you have to take plenty of notes because those topics are vast. Well, do you need to study more? <br/>\n<span class=\"strong\">Kyle</span>: Yes, Mandy. Before doing my presentation, I need to understand the concept of structured programming. <br/>", "image": "enpe7n2le02ob11re2img02.jpg", "id": 1}, {"time": "72500", "text": "<span class=\"strong\">Mandy</span>: Ok, just let me tell you I always have to make my best to explain this difficult topic. I'll try anyway. The structured programming refers to a set of principles or pre-established tasks and subroutines. This transfers tasks easier so, the understanding of software and its performance is also easier. <br/>\n<span class=\"strong\">Kyle</span>: I think I get it now! I usually need to research more or ask for help to understand. But you didn't confuse me. Ok and the last one, what are the principles of structured programming? <br/>\n<span class=\"strong\">Mandy</span>: Well, structured programming is based on principles or pre-established tasks like branching statements as If . . . Then . . . Else, loops, modularization which makes it easier to realize which part of a program is causing problems, and information hiding. It keeps information and during the procedure, it can't be damaged by any other program part.", "image": "enpe7n2le02ob11re2img03.jpg", "id": 2}], "audiosrc": "enpe7n2le02ob11re2aud01"}, "type": "slideshow"}]}], "id": 0, "type": "scholar"}]}